@startuml Deal Management Application - Sequence Diagram

' Define the styles
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam participantBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam actorBackgroundColor #fff3e0
skinparam actorBorderColor #f57c00
skinparam databaseBackgroundColor #f3e5f5
skinparam databaseBorderColor #7b1fa2

' Title
title Deal Management Application - Create Deal Sequence (Runtime Behavior)

' Participants
actor "Sales Rep" as user
participant "Tomcat\nWeb Server" as tomcat <<Container>>
participant "DealController" as controller <<Component>>
participant "JsonHelper" as json <<Utility>>
participant "DealService" as service <<Component>>
participant "H2DealRepository" as repository <<Component>>
participant "DatabaseManager" as dbManager <<Component>>
database "H2 Database" as db <<Database>>

' Sequence flow for creating a new deal
== HTTP Request Processing ==

user -> tomcat : POST /api/v1/integration/deals\nContent-Type: application/json\nBody: {"title": "New Deal", "salesRepId": "USER-123", ...}
activate tomcat
note right
  <b>Step 1: HTTP Request</b>

  Sales rep sends HTTP POST
  with JSON payload containing
  deal information.

  HTTP Method: POST
  Endpoint: /api/v1/integration/deals
end note

tomcat -> controller : doPost(request, response)
activate controller
note right
  <b>Step 2: Servlet Routing</b>

  Tomcat routes request to
  DealController based on
  URL mapping.

  Servlet mapping:
  /api/v1/integration/deals/*
  → DealController
end note

== Request Parsing ==

controller -> controller : readRequestBody(request)
activate controller
note right
  <b>Step 3: Read Request Body</b>

  Controller reads HTTP request
  body stream into String.

  Reads: InputStream → String
end note
controller --> controller : requestBody (JSON String)
deactivate controller

controller -> json : fromJson(requestBody, Deal.class)
activate json
note right
  <b>Step 4: JSON Deserialization</b>

  Convert JSON string to
  Deal domain object.

  Uses Gson library for:
  • JSON parsing
  • Object instantiation
  • Property mapping
end note
json --> controller : deal (Deal object)
deactivate json

== Business Logic Validation ==

controller -> service : createDeal(deal)
activate service
note right
  <b>Step 5: Delegate to Service</b>

  Controller delegates to service layer.

  <b>Separation of Concerns:</b>
  Controller handles HTTP only,
  service handles business logic.
end note

service -> service : validateDeal(deal)
activate service
note right
  <b>Step 6: Business Validation</b>

  Service validates business rules:
  • Title is required
  • Sales rep ID is required
  • At least one product
  • Total value > 0

  <b>Throws:</b>
  IllegalArgumentException
  if validation fails
end note
service --> service : validation passed
deactivate service

service -> service : Set status to OPEN\nif not specified
note right
  <b>Step 7: Apply Business Defaults</b>

  Service sets default values
  based on business rules.

  Default status: OPEN
  (new deals start as OPEN)
end note

== Data Persistence ==

service -> repository : save(deal)
activate repository
note right
  <b>Step 8: Delegate to Repository</b>

  Service delegates data persistence
  to repository layer.

  <b>Abstraction:</b>
  Service doesn't know it's H2,
  only knows Repository<Deal> interface.
end note

repository -> repository : Check if deal.getId() is null
note right
  <b>Step 9: Insert vs Update</b>

  Repository determines operation:
  • null ID → insert()
  • existing ID → update()

  This example: ID is null → insert
end note

repository -> repository : generateId()
activate repository
note right
  <b>Step 10: Generate Unique ID</b>

  Repository generates unique ID:
  DEAL-{UUID}

  Example:
  DEAL-a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890...
end note
repository --> repository : "DEAL-{UUID}"
deactivate repository

repository -> repository : insert(deal)
activate repository
note right
  <b>Step 11: Prepare SQL INSERT</b>

  Repository prepares SQL statement
  with PreparedStatement.

  SQL:
  INSERT INTO deals (id, title, ...)
  VALUES (?, ?, ...)
end note

repository -> json : toJson(deal.getProducts())
activate json
note right
  <b>Step 12: Serialize Products</b>

  Convert products list to JSON
  for database storage.

  List<DealProduct> →
  JSON string
end note
json --> repository : productsJson
deactivate json

repository -> dbManager : getConnection()
activate dbManager
note right
  <b>Step 13: Get Database Connection</b>

  DatabaseManager provides
  JDBC connection from pool.

  Singleton pattern ensures
  one connection pool.
end note
dbManager --> repository : connection
deactivate dbManager

repository -> db : executeUpdate(\nINSERT INTO deals ...)
activate db
note right
  <b>Step 14: Execute SQL INSERT</b>

  JDBC executes parameterized
  SQL INSERT statement.

  <b>Security:</b>
  PreparedStatement prevents
  SQL injection attacks.

  <b>Atomicity:</b>
  Single transaction for insert.
end note
db --> repository : rowsAffected = 1
deactivate db

repository --> repository : Log success
repository --> service : deal (with ID)
deactivate repository

== Response Formation ==

service --> service : Log creation
service --> controller : createdDeal
deactivate service
note right
  <b>Step 15: Return Created Deal</b>

  Service returns saved deal
  with generated ID back to controller.
end note

controller -> json : toJson(createdDeal)
activate json
note right
  <b>Step 16: JSON Serialization</b>

  Convert Deal object to
  JSON string for HTTP response.

  Deal → JSON string
end note
json --> controller : responseJson
deactivate json

controller -> controller : sendJsonResponse(\nresponse, createdDeal,\nSC_CREATED)
activate controller
note right
  <b>Step 17: Format HTTP Response</b>

  Controller sets:
  • Status: 201 Created
  • Content-Type: application/json
  • Body: JSON representation
end note
controller --> controller : response configured
deactivate controller

controller -> tomcat : response
deactivate controller
note right
  <b>Step 18: Return HTTP Response</b>

  Controller returns response
  to Tomcat for sending.
end note

tomcat -> user : HTTP 201 Created\nContent-Type: application/json\nBody: {"id": "DEAL-...", ...}
deactivate tomcat
note right
  <b>Step 19: HTTP Response Sent</b>

  Tomcat sends HTTP response
  back to client.

  Status: 201 Created
  Body: Created deal with ID
end note

== Complete ==

note over user, db
  <b>Transaction Complete</b>

  Deal successfully created and persisted.

  <b>Key Points:</b>
  1. Request flows through layers: Controller → Service → Repository
  2. Each layer has distinct responsibility
  3. Validation happens in service layer
  4. JSON conversion at boundaries (HTTP ↔ Domain, Domain ↔ Database)
  5. Response flows back through same layers
  6. HTTP status code (201) indicates successful creation
end note

legend right
  <b>Sequence Diagram - Create Deal Operation</b>

  Shows runtime interaction between components
  when creating a new deal via REST API.

  <b>Layers Traversed:</b>
  1. Presentation (Controller) - HTTP handling
  2. Business Logic (Service) - Validation & rules
  3. Data Access (Repository) - Persistence
  4. Database - Storage

  <b>Key Patterns Demonstrated:</b>
  • <b>Layered Architecture:</b> Clear separation of concerns
  • <b>Request/Response Flow:</b> Unidirectional through layers
  • <b>Dependency Injection:</b> Each layer receives next layer
  • <b>Repository Pattern:</b> Data access abstraction
  • <b>Service Layer:</b> Business logic encapsulation

  <b>Data Transformations:</b>
  1. HTTP JSON → Deal object (request)
  2. Deal object → SQL parameters (persistence)
  3. List<DealProduct> → JSON (storage)
  4. Deal object → HTTP JSON (response)

  <b>Error Handling:</b>
  • Validation errors → 400 Bad Request
  • Business rule violations → 409 Conflict
  • Database errors → 500 Internal Server Error

  <b>HTTP Status Codes:</b>
  • 201 Created - Successful creation (shown)
  • 400 Bad Request - Validation failure
  • 500 Internal Server Error - Unexpected error

  <b>Sequence Type:</b>
  POST request (create operation)
  Other sequences: GET, PUT, DELETE follow similar pattern
endlegend

@enduml